    {
      "name": "formats-json/duplicate-keys/later-scalar-replaces-an-earlier-array",
      "spec": "docs/07-codecs-and-deserialization.md#71-two-stages",
      "operation": "parse",
      "purpose": "edge-case",
      "input": {
        "format": "json",
        "text": "{\"a\": [1, 2], \"a\": 3}"
      },
      "expect": {
        "ok": true,
        "document": {
          "edges": [
            [
              "a",
              {
                "scalar": {
                  "kind": "integer",
                  "value": 3
                }
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "comment": "C-11 against the array rule: `\"a\": [1, 2]` alone is two edges (a repeated label), but a later `\"a\": 3` is a duplicate key and replaces the whole earlier value, so the result is the single edge `(a,3)`. A reader that appends the later key to the run would produce three edges."
    },
    {
      "name": "formats-json/duplicate-keys/array-under-one-key-is-not-a-duplicate",
      "spec": "docs/07-codecs-and-deserialization.md#71-two-stages",
